IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `whs-dates`

-- Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (NSW) -- the provisions a worker meets who is hurt at work, is put at risk, or is
-- treated badly for raising safety: who is a worker, what must be notified and preserved after an incident, the right
-- to stop unsafe work, the civil claim for discriminatory or coercive conduct (Part 6), the offences and penalties that
-- follow a breach, asking the regulator to prosecute, the limitation period for prosecutions, and what the Act does and
-- does not do for a civil claim for damages (s 267).
--
-- SEAT: the worker (s 7), including a prospective worker or another person treated adversely under Part 6, and anyone
-- (often the injured worker or the family of a worker who died) who asks the regulator to prosecute under s 231. Every
-- exported question is one that person would ask: "Am I a worker?", "Was my injury a serious injury?", "Must my
-- employer tell the regulator?", "May I stop unsafe work?", "Can I take my employer to the Industrial Court for
-- punishing me for raising safety, and by when?", "What can the court order?", "What offence was it, and what is the
-- most the business can be fined?", "Can I ask for a prosecution, and must the regulator answer me?", "Can I sue under
-- this Act?". The regulator, the inspector, the Director of Public Prosecutions and the courts get no seat: what they do
-- is encoded as what it means for the worker.
--
-- Provisions: ss 5, 7 (business or undertaking; worker); ss 30-34 and Part 2A (ss 34B-34F) (offences, industrial
-- manslaughter); Part 3 (ss 35-39) (notifiable incidents, notice, records, site preservation); Part 5 Division 6
-- (ss 83-88) (ceasing unsafe work); Part 6 (ss 104-115) (discriminatory, coercive and misleading conduct; civil and
-- criminal routes); ss 229B, 230, 231, 232 (court, who prosecutes, request to prosecute, limitation); ss 242B, 242C
-- (penalty units); s 267 (civil liability); ss 272, 273 (no contracting out; no levy on workers). Out, with reasons:
-- NOTES.md, "Scope".
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no question answers JUST TRUE; JUST FALSE where the Act's own words say no (a "means" definition, a
-- condition introduced by "if", "where" or "unless", a flat "must not", "cannot" or "does not apply", a period the Act
-- fixes); or NOTHING where the Act does not answer on the facts given. Each NOTHING carries a "NOTHING-kind" comment.
-- Judgments the Act leaves to a court or to someone else (reasonable concern, gross negligence, reasonable excuse, a
-- substantial or dominant reason) are asked as inputs and answered conditionally ("if the court finds ...").
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; version as at 2 March 2025; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for
-- public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Work Health and Safety Act 2011`

§§ `The nouns`

-- s 36(a)-(c): the treatment an injury or illness required. "includes any other injury or illness prescribed by the
-- regulations" is the last entry; the Work Health and Safety Regulation 2017 is not deposited, so that is asked.
DECLARE `The treatment my injury or illness required` IS ONE OF
    `immediate treatment as an in-patient in a hospital`
    `immediate treatment for the amputation of any part of the body`
    `immediate treatment for a serious head injury`
    `immediate treatment for a serious eye injury`
    `immediate treatment for a serious burn`
    `immediate treatment for the separation of skin from underlying tissue, such as degloving or scalping`
    `immediate treatment for a spinal injury`
    `immediate treatment for the loss of a bodily function`
    `immediate treatment for serious lacerations`
    `medical treatment after exposure to a substance`
    `an injury or illness of a kind the regulations prescribe as serious`
    `some other treatment, or none`

-- s 37(a)-(l): the kinds of event a dangerous incident involves.
DECLARE `What the incident involved` IS ONE OF
    `an uncontrolled escape, spillage or leakage of a substance`
    `an uncontrolled implosion, explosion or fire`
    `an uncontrolled escape of gas or steam`
    `an uncontrolled escape of a pressurised substance`
    `electric shock`
    `the fall or release from a height of any plant, substance or thing`
    `the collapse, overturning, failure or malfunction of, or damage to, plant that must be authorised for use`
    `the collapse or partial collapse of a structure`
    `the collapse or failure of an excavation or its shoring`
    `the inrush of water, mud or gas in workings, an underground excavation or a tunnel`
    `the interruption of the main ventilation in an underground excavation or tunnel`
    `another event the regulations prescribe`
    `none of these events`

-- The answer to "must the business notify the regulator?" (s 38).
DECLARE `What section 38 requires of the business` IS ONE OF
    `the business must notify the regulator immediately after becoming aware, by the fastest possible means, by telephone or in writing`
    `no notice is required, because the workplace is one the mines and petroleum sites Act applies to`
    `no notice is required, because it was not a notifiable incident`
    `no notice is required, because the incident did not arise out of the conduct of the business`

-- s 39(3): actions the duty to preserve the site does not prevent, and anything else.
DECLARE `The action at the incident site` IS ONE OF
    `assisting an injured person`
    `removing a deceased person`
    `action essential to make the site safe or to minimise the risk of a further notifiable incident`
    `action associated with a police investigation`
    `action an inspector or the regulator has permitted`
    `any other action`

-- The answer to "what must I do after I stop work?" (s 86).
DECLARE `What I must do after ceasing work` IS ONE OF
    `notify the business as soon as practicable that I have ceased work, and remain available to carry out suitable alternative work`
    `remain available to carry out suitable alternative work (having ceased on a representative's direction, I need not notify the business)`
    `section 86 puts no duty on me, because I did not cease work under Division 6`

-- s 105: who the person asking is, to the one who acted.
DECLARE `Who I am to the person who acted` IS ONE OF
    `a worker of theirs`
    `a prospective worker`
    `someone in, or seeking, a commercial arrangement with them`

-- s 105(1)(a)-(d): the conduct.
DECLARE `What was done to me` IS ONE OF
    `I was dismissed`
    `my contract for services was terminated`
    `I was put to my detriment in my engagement`
    `my position was altered to my detriment`
    `I was refused or not offered engagement`
    `I was treated less favourably than another prospective worker in the terms offered`
    `a commercial arrangement with me was terminated`
    `they refused or failed to enter into a commercial arrangement with me`
    `something else`

-- s 106(a)-(j): the circumstances that make a reason prohibited.
DECLARE `The circumstance behind the conduct` IS ONE OF
    `I am, have been or propose to be a health and safety representative or committee member`
    `I undertake, have undertaken or propose to undertake another role under the Act`
    `I exercise or perform, have exercised or performed, or propose to exercise or perform a power or function as a representative or committee member`
    `I exercise, have exercised or propose to exercise a power under the Act, or in a particular way`
    `I perform, have performed or propose to perform a function under the Act, or in a particular way`
    `I refrain, have refrained or propose to refrain from exercising a power or performing a function under the Act`
    `I assist, or give information to, someone exercising a power or performing a function under the Act`
    `I raise, have raised or propose to raise a work health and safety issue or concern with someone section 106(h) lists`
    `I am, have been or propose to be involved in resolving a work health and safety issue under the Act`
    `I am taking, have taken or propose to take action to seek compliance with a duty or obligation under the Act`
    `none of these reasons`

-- s 112(2): the conduct the Industrial Court may make orders about.
DECLARE `The conduct I complain of` IS ONE OF
    `discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason`
    `requesting, instructing, inducing, encouraging, authorising or assisting discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason`
    `coercion or inducement contrary to section 108`
    `none of these kinds of conduct`

-- s 112(6): who is an eligible person.
DECLARE `My standing` IS ONE OF
    `I am a person affected by the contravention`
    `I am a representative authorised by a person affected`
    `neither`

-- The orders of ss 111 and 112(3).
DECLARE `Order` IS ONE OF
    `an injunction`
    `an order declaring that the person engaged in the conduct`
    `an order that the person pay me compensation within a specified period`
    `an order that I be reinstated or re-employed in my former or a similar position, or employed in the position I applied for or a similar one`
    `any other order the Industrial Court considers appropriate`

-- The offences a worker meets, for the penalty, court and limitation questions.
DECLARE `The offence` IS ONE OF
    `industrial manslaughter (s 34C)`
    `a Category 1 offence (s 31)`
    `a Category 2 offence (s 32)`
    `a Category 3 offence (s 33)`
    `failing to notify a notifiable incident (s 38(1))`
    `failing to keep the record of a notifiable incident (s 38(7))`
    `failing to preserve an incident site (s 39(1))`
    `discriminatory conduct, or requesting or assisting it (s 104 or 107)`
    `coercion or inducement (s 108)`
    `misrepresentation about rights under the Act (s 109)`
    `levying a worker for health and safety (s 273)`

-- The offender, as the penalty provisions divide them.
DECLARE `The offender` IS ONE OF
    `an individual conducting the business or undertaking, or an officer of it`
    `any other individual`
    `a body corporate`

-- The answer to "which offence was it?" (ss 31-34, 34C, 34D).
DECLARE `The most serious offence on these facts` IS ONE OF
    `industrial manslaughter`
    `a Category 1 offence`
    `a Category 2 offence`
    `a Category 3 offence`
    `no offence under Part 2 or Part 2A on these facts`

-- The answer to "which court?" (s 229B).
DECLARE `How the prosecution is dealt with` IS ONE OF
    `on indictment`
    `summarily, before the Local Court or the Industrial Court`
    `summarily, before the Local Court or the Industrial Court, unless the prosecutor elects to proceed on indictment`

-- The answer to "by when must the prosecution be brought?" (ss 232, 34E).
DECLARE `The time limit for the prosecution` IS ONE OF
    `there is no time limit, because it is industrial manslaughter`
    `the prosecution must be brought by` HAS `the closing day` IS A DATE
    `the period has ended, but a Category 1 prosecution may still be brought on fresh evidence the court accepts`

-- The answer to "was it done in time?" for the two regulative rules.
DECLARE `Whether it was done in time` IS ONE OF
    `done in time`
    `done late`
    `not done, and the time has passed`
    `not done yet, and the time has not passed`
    `no time limit applies, because it was not required`

-- The parties and acts of the regulative rules.
DECLARE `WHS party` IS ONE OF `the business`, `the regulator`
DECLARE `WHS act` IS ONE OF
    `give the written notice of the incident`
    `advise the person who asked in writing`

§§ `ss 5, 7 -- business or undertaking, and worker`

-- s 7(1) "A person is a worker if the person carries out work in any capacity for a person conducting a business or
-- undertaking, including work as" an employee, contractor, subcontractor, their employees, labour hire employee,
-- outworker, apprentice, trainee, student on work experience, volunteer or a prescribed class. s 7(2): a police officer
-- is a worker. s 7(3): the individual conducting the business who carries out work in it is also a worker. A "means"-
-- type definition introduced by "if": none of the three routes is JUST FALSE.
@export Am I a worker under the Work Health and Safety Act (s 7)?
GIVEN `I carry out work for a business or undertaking` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you carry out work in any capacity for a person conducting a business or undertaking: as an employee, contractor or subcontractor (or their employee), labour hire employee, outworker, apprentice or trainee, student on work experience, volunteer, or otherwise
      `I conduct the business myself and work in it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the individual who conducts the business or undertaking and you carry out work in it yourself
      `I am a police officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a police officer (you are at work while on duty or lawfully performing police functions)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I a worker` MEANS
    IF `I carry out work for a business or undertaking` OR `I conduct the business myself and work in it` OR `I am a police officer`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 5(1) "a person conducts a business or undertaking (a) whether ... alone or with others, and (b) whether or not ...
-- for profit or gain"; s 5(4) "does not conduct a business or undertaking to the extent that the person is engaged
-- solely as a worker in, or as an officer of" it; s 5(5) an elected member of a local authority "does not in that
-- capacity"; s 5(7) "A volunteer association does not conduct a business or undertaking" (s 5(8): volunteers working
-- together for community purposes, none of whom employs anyone to work for it). Flat negatives: JUST FALSE. "Business"
-- and "undertaking" are not defined: whether there is one is asked; where there is not, NOTHING.
@export Is the one I work for a "person conducting a business or undertaking", who owes me the duties of the Act (s 5)?
GIVEN `they conduct a business or undertaking` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person or organisation you work for conducts a business or undertaking, alone or with others, for profit or not (a partnership or unincorporated association counts)
      `it is a volunteer association` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a group of volunteers working together for community purposes, none of whom, alone or jointly, employs anyone to carry out work for it
      `they are an elected council member acting as such` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is an elected member of a local council, county council or joint organisation acting in that capacity
      `they are engaged solely as a worker or officer of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is engaged solely as a worker in, or an officer of, the business (and does not conduct it)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the one I work for a person conducting a business or undertaking` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `it is a volunteer association` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `they are an elected council member acting as such` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `they are engaged solely as a worker or officer of it` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `they conduct a business or undertaking` THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term: "business" and "undertaking" are not defined)

§§ `Part 3 -- incident notification (ss 35-39)`

-- s 36 "serious injury or illness of a person means an injury or illness requiring the person to have (a) immediate
-- treatment as an in-patient in a hospital, or (b) immediate treatment for [eight kinds], or (c) medical treatment
-- within 48 hours of exposure to a substance, and includes any other injury or illness prescribed by the regulations
-- but does not include an illness or injury of a prescribed kind." A "means" definition: outside it, JUST FALSE.
-- s 4 "medical treatment means treatment by a medical practitioner". "Within 48 hours" includes the 48th hour.
GIVEN `the treatment` IS A `The treatment my injury or illness required`
      `the hours from the exposure to the medical treatment` IS A NUMBER
      `the regulations exclude it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the injury is serious` `the treatment` `the hours from the exposure to the medical treatment` `the regulations exclude it` IF
        (NOT `the regulations exclude it`)
    AND CONSIDER `the treatment`
        WHEN `medical treatment after exposure to a substance` THEN `the hours from the exposure to the medical treatment` AT MOST 48
        WHEN `some other treatment, or none` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

@export Is my injury or illness a "serious injury or illness" under the Act (s 36)?
GIVEN `the treatment my injury or illness required` IS A `The treatment my injury or illness required` @desc The treatment your injury or illness required. One of: "immediate treatment as an in-patient in a hospital", "immediate treatment for the amputation of any part of the body", "immediate treatment for a serious head injury", "immediate treatment for a serious eye injury", "immediate treatment for a serious burn", "immediate treatment for the separation of skin from underlying tissue, such as degloving or scalping", "immediate treatment for a spinal injury", "immediate treatment for the loss of a bodily function", "immediate treatment for serious lacerations", "medical treatment after exposure to a substance", "an injury or illness of a kind the regulations prescribe as serious", "some other treatment, or none"
      `the hours from the exposure to the medical treatment` IS A NUMBER @desc If you were treated by a doctor after exposure to a substance, how many hours after the exposure the treatment began (any number otherwise)
      `the regulations exclude my kind of injury or illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Work Health and Safety Regulation excludes your kind of injury or illness from "serious injury or illness"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my injury or illness a serious injury or illness` MEANS
    IF `the injury is serious` `the treatment my injury or illness required` `the hours from the exposure to the medical treatment` `the regulations exclude my kind of injury or illness`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 37 "a dangerous incident means an incident in relation to a workplace that exposes a worker or any other person to
-- a serious risk to a person's health or safety emanating from an immediate or imminent exposure to" (a)-(l), "but does
-- not include an incident of a prescribed kind". A "means" definition.
GIVEN `what it involved` IS A `What the incident involved`
      `it exposed someone to a serious risk from an immediate or imminent exposure` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the regulations exclude the incident` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the incident is dangerous` `what it involved` `it exposed someone to a serious risk from an immediate or imminent exposure` `the regulations exclude the incident` IF
        (NOT `the regulations exclude the incident`)
    AND `it exposed someone to a serious risk from an immediate or imminent exposure`
    AND (NOT (`what it involved` EQUALS `none of these events`))

@export Was the incident at my workplace a "dangerous incident" under the Act (s 37)?
GIVEN `what the incident involved` IS A `What the incident involved` @desc What the incident involved. One of: "an uncontrolled escape, spillage or leakage of a substance", "an uncontrolled implosion, explosion or fire", "an uncontrolled escape of gas or steam", "an uncontrolled escape of a pressurised substance", "electric shock", "the fall or release from a height of any plant, substance or thing", "the collapse, overturning, failure or malfunction of, or damage to, plant that must be authorised for use", "the collapse or partial collapse of a structure", "the collapse or failure of an excavation or its shoring", "the inrush of water, mud or gas in workings, an underground excavation or a tunnel", "the interruption of the main ventilation in an underground excavation or tunnel", "another event the regulations prescribe", "none of these events"
      `it exposed someone to a serious risk from an immediate or imminent exposure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the incident exposed you or anyone else to a serious risk to health or safety coming from an immediate or imminent exposure to that event
      `the regulations exclude the incident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Work Health and Safety Regulation excludes this kind of incident
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was it a dangerous incident` MEANS
    IF `the incident is dangerous` `what the incident involved` `it exposed someone to a serious risk from an immediate or imminent exposure` `the regulations exclude the incident`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 35 "notifiable incident means (a) the death of a person, or (b) a serious injury or illness of a person, or (c) a
-- dangerous incident." s 38(1) "A person who conducts a business or undertaking must ensure that the regulator is
-- notified immediately after becoming aware that a notifiable incident arising out of the conduct of the business or
-- undertaking has occurred." s 38(2), (3): by the fastest possible means, by telephone or in writing. s 38(8): no
-- notice for a workplace to which the Work Health and Safety (Mines and Petroleum Sites) Act 2013 applies.
@export Must the business I work for notify the regulator of what happened to me, and how (ss 35-38)?
GIVEN `someone died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a person died
      `the treatment my injury or illness required` IS A `The treatment my injury or illness required` @desc The treatment the injury or illness required (as in the serious-injury question; "some other treatment, or none" if no one was hurt)
      `the hours from the exposure to the medical treatment` IS A NUMBER @desc If treated by a doctor after exposure to a substance, how many hours after the exposure (any number otherwise)
      `the regulations exclude my kind of injury or illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Work Health and Safety Regulation excludes this kind of injury or illness
      `what the incident involved` IS A `What the incident involved` @desc What the incident involved (as in the dangerous-incident question; "none of these events" if nothing on that list)
      `it exposed someone to a serious risk from an immediate or imminent exposure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the incident exposed anyone to a serious risk from an immediate or imminent exposure
      `the regulations exclude the incident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Work Health and Safety Regulation excludes this kind of incident
      `it arose out of the conduct of the business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the incident arose out of the conduct of the business or undertaking
      `the workplace is a mine or petroleum site` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the workplace is one the Work Health and Safety (Mines and Petroleum Sites) Act 2013 applies to
GIVETH A `What section 38 requires of the business`
`Must the business notify the regulator` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the workplace is a mine or petroleum site` THEN `no notice is required, because the workplace is one the mines and petroleum sites Act applies to`
           IF (NOT `notifiable`) THEN `no notice is required, because it was not a notifiable incident`
           IF (NOT `it arose out of the conduct of the business`) THEN `no notice is required, because the incident did not arise out of the conduct of the business`
           OTHERWISE `the business must notify the regulator immediately after becoming aware, by the fastest possible means, by telephone or in writing`
    WHERE
        `notifiable` MEANS
               `someone died`
            OR `the injury is serious` `the treatment my injury or illness required` `the hours from the exposure to the medical treatment` `the regulations exclude my kind of injury or illness`
            OR `the incident is dangerous` `what the incident involved` `it exposed someone to a serious risk from an immediate or imminent exposure` `the regulations exclude the incident`

-- s 38(4) "A person giving notice by telephone must ... (b) if required by the regulator, give a written notice of the
-- incident within 48 hours of that requirement being made." Time 0 is the hour the regulator made the requirement; the
-- unit is hours. The 48th hour itself is in time.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `WHS party` `WHS act`
`s 38(4)(b) -- the business must give the written notice within 48 hours of the regulator's requirement` MEANS
    PARTY `the business`
    MUST `give the written notice of the incident`
    WITHIN 48
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the business` BECAUSE "written notice of the notifiable incident not given within 48 hours of the regulator requiring it (s 38(4)(b))"

@export If the regulator required a written notice of my incident after a telephone notice, was it (or can it still be) given in time (s 38(4)(b))?
GIVEN `the regulator required a written notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the regulator, after the business notified by telephone, required a written notice of the incident
      `the business has given the written notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business has given the written notice
      `the hours from the requirement to the written notice` IS A NUMBER @desc If it has, how many hours after the regulator's requirement it gave the written notice (any number otherwise)
      `the hours since the requirement` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since the regulator made the requirement
GIVETH A `Whether it was done in time`
`Was the written notice of my incident given in time` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the regulator required a written notice`)
    THEN `no time limit applies, because it was not required`
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 38(4)(b) -- the business must give the written notice within 48 hours of the regulator's requirement` 0 `the events`
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `done in time`
         OTHERWISE
             BRANCH IF `the business has given the written notice` THEN `done late`
                    IF `the hours since the requirement` GREATER THAN 48 THEN `not done, and the time has passed`
                    OTHERWISE `not done yet, and the time has not passed`
    WHERE
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `the business has given the written notice`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the business` `give the written notice of the incident` `the hours from the requirement to the written notice`)
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL `the hours since the requirement`)

-- s 38(7) "must keep a record of each notifiable incident for at least 5 years from the day that notice of the incident
-- is given to the regulator". The last day is the same day five years on (fork F1).
@export Until what day must the business keep its record of my notifiable incident (s 38(7))?
GIVEN `the day notice was given to the regulator` IS A DATE @desc The day the business gave the regulator notice of the incident
GIVETH A DATE
`Until what day must the business keep the record of my incident` MEANS
    `the day notice was given to the regulator` `plus years` 5

-- s 39(1) "The person with management or control of a workplace at which a notifiable incident has occurred must
-- ensure so far as is reasonably practicable, that the site where the incident occurred is not disturbed until an
-- inspector arrives at the site or any earlier time that an inspector directs." s 39(3): the duty "does not prevent"
-- the five listed actions; s 39(4): it "does not apply" to a mines or petroleum site. An "until" bar: JUST TRUE before
-- the inspector arrives (or the earlier time directed), JUST FALSE after. "Where a notifiable incident has occurred"
-- is asked as a fact (the notification question works it out); fork F6.
@export Must the site where I was hurt be left undisturbed, for the action now proposed (s 39)?
GIVEN `a notifiable incident occurred there` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a death, serious injury or illness, or dangerous incident (a notifiable incident) occurred at the site
      `the workplace is a mine or petroleum site` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the workplace is one the Work Health and Safety (Mines and Petroleum Sites) Act 2013 applies to
      `an inspector has arrived or the time an inspector directed has come` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an inspector has arrived at the site, or an earlier time an inspector directed has come
      `the action proposed` IS A `The action at the incident site` @desc The action proposed at the site. One of: "assisting an injured person", "removing a deceased person", "action essential to make the site safe or to minimise the risk of a further notifiable incident", "action associated with a police investigation", "action an inspector or the regulator has permitted", "any other action"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the site be left undisturbed` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the workplace is a mine or petroleum site` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (NOT `a notifiable incident occurred there`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `an inspector has arrived or the time an inspector directed has come` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (NOT (`the action proposed` EQUALS `any other action`)) THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE  -- so far as is reasonably practicable (s 39(1))

§§ `Part 5 Division 6 -- stopping unsafe work (ss 83-88)`

-- s 84 "A worker may cease, or refuse to carry out, work if the worker has a reasonable concern that to carry out the
-- work would expose the worker to a serious risk to the worker's health or safety, emanating from an immediate or
-- imminent exposure to a hazard." s 85(1): a health and safety representative may direct a worker in the work group to
-- cease work on the same concern; s 85(2), (3): only after consulting and trying to resolve it, unless the risk is so
-- serious and immediate or imminent that consulting first is not reasonable; s 85(6): only if the representative has
-- the training. Whether a concern is reasonable is a judgment, asked. Not a worker: s 84 does not speak (NOTHING).
@export May I stop or refuse to do work I think is unsafe (ss 84, 85)?
GIVEN `I am a worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker (see the worker question)
      `I have a reasonable concern of a serious risk from an immediate or imminent hazard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a reasonable concern that doing the work would expose you to a serious risk to your health or safety from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard
      `my health and safety representative directed me to cease work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the health and safety representative for your work group directed you to cease the work
      `the representative had the required training` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that representative has completed the initial training prescribed for representatives (or equivalent training, or did so for another work group)
      `the representative consulted first, or the risk was too serious and immediate to consult` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the representative first consulted the business and tried to resolve the matter as an issue, or the risk was so serious and immediate or imminent that it was not reasonable to consult first
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I cease unsafe work` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `I am a worker`) THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (scope limit: s 84 speaks of "a worker", without "only")
           IF `I have a reasonable concern of a serious risk from an immediate or imminent hazard` THEN JUST TRUE
           IF     `my health and safety representative directed me to cease work`
              AND `the representative had the required training`
              AND `the representative consulted first, or the risk was too serious and immediate to consult`
           THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- s 86 "A worker who ceases work under this Division must (a) as soon as practicable, notify the person conducting the
-- business or undertaking that the worker has ceased work under this Division unless the worker ceased work under a
-- direction from a health and safety representative, and (b) remain available to carry out suitable alternative work."
@export What must I do after I stop unsafe work (s 86)?
GIVEN `I ceased work under Division 6` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you ceased or refused work under the right to stop unsafe work (s 84) or on a representative's direction (s 85)
      `I ceased on a representative's direction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you ceased work because a health and safety representative directed you to
GIVETH A `What I must do after ceasing work`
`What must I do after I cease work` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `I ceased work under Division 6`) THEN `section 86 puts no duty on me, because I did not cease work under Division 6`
           IF `I ceased on a representative's direction` THEN `remain available to carry out suitable alternative work (having ceased on a representative's direction, I need not notify the business)`
           OTHERWISE `notify the business as soon as practicable that I have ceased work, and remain available to carry out suitable alternative work`

-- s 88 "If a worker ceases work under this Division, that action does not affect the continuity of engagement of the
-- worker for prescribed purposes if the worker has not unreasonably failed to comply with a direction to carry out
-- suitable alternative work (a) at the same or another workplace, and (b) that was safe and appropriate". The
-- "prescribed purposes" are in the Regulation (not deposited). "If ... not unreasonably failed": a failed "if" is
-- JUST FALSE for this section's protection. Not having ceased under the Division: NOTHING.
@export If I stopped unsafe work, does section 88 protect the continuity of my engagement?
GIVEN `I ceased work under Division 6` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you ceased or refused work under the right to stop unsafe work (s 84) or on a representative's direction (s 85)
      `I unreasonably failed to do suitable alternative work I was directed to` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you unreasonably failed to comply with a direction to carry out suitable alternative work, at the same or another workplace, that was safe and appropriate for you
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does section 88 protect the continuity of my engagement` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `I ceased work under Division 6`) THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (scope limit: s 88 speaks only of ceasing under the Division)
           IF `I unreasonably failed to do suitable alternative work I was directed to` THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `Part 6 -- discriminatory, coercive and misleading conduct (ss 104-115)`

-- s 105(1) "a person engages in discriminatory conduct if (a) the person (i) dismisses a worker, or (ii) terminates a
-- contract for services with a worker, or (iii) puts a worker to his or her detriment in the engagement of the worker,
-- or (iv) alters the position of a worker to the worker's detriment, or (b) ... (i) refuses or fails to offer to engage
-- a prospective worker, or (ii) treats a prospective worker less favourably ... in offering terms of engagement, or
-- (c) ... terminates a commercial arrangement with another person, or (d) ... refuses or fails to enter into a
-- commercial arrangement with another person." s 105(2): organising, or threatening to organise or take, the action
-- also counts. A definition introduced by "if": outside it, JUST FALSE.
@export Was what was done (or threatened) to me "discriminatory conduct" under the Act (s 105)?
GIVEN `who I am to them` IS A `Who I am to the person who acted` @desc Who you are to the person who acted. One of: "a worker of theirs", "a prospective worker", "someone in, or seeking, a commercial arrangement with them"
      `what was done to me` IS A `What was done to me` @desc What they did, organised, or threatened to do or organise. One of: "I was dismissed", "my contract for services was terminated", "I was put to my detriment in my engagement", "my position was altered to my detriment", "I was refused or not offered engagement", "I was treated less favourably than another prospective worker in the terms offered", "a commercial arrangement with me was terminated", "they refused or failed to enter into a commercial arrangement with me", "something else"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is it discriminatory conduct` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what was done to me`
    WHEN `I was dismissed` THEN `as a worker`
    WHEN `my contract for services was terminated` THEN `as a worker`
    WHEN `I was put to my detriment in my engagement` THEN `as a worker`
    WHEN `my position was altered to my detriment` THEN `as a worker`
    WHEN `I was refused or not offered engagement` THEN `as a prospective worker`
    WHEN `I was treated less favourably than another prospective worker in the terms offered` THEN `as a prospective worker`
    WHEN `a commercial arrangement with me was terminated` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `they refused or failed to enter into a commercial arrangement with me` THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `as a worker` MEANS IF `who I am to them` EQUALS `a worker of theirs` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE
        `as a prospective worker` MEANS IF `who I am to them` EQUALS `a prospective worker` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 106 conduct "is engaged in for a prohibited reason if it is engaged in because" of one of (a)-(j). s 112(4): in the
-- civil proceeding "a person may be found to have engaged in discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason only if a
-- reason referred to in section 106 was a substantial reason for the conduct". s 113(2): "if a prohibited reason is
-- alleged ... that reason is presumed to be a substantial reason ... unless the defendant proves, on the balance of
-- probabilities, that the reason was not a substantial reason". Not alleged: the Act does not answer (NOTHING).
@export In my claim to the Industrial Court, is the conduct taken to have been for a prohibited reason (ss 106, 112(4), 113(2))?
GIVEN `the circumstance` IS A `The circumstance behind the conduct` @desc Why you say it was done to you. One of: "I am, have been or propose to be a health and safety representative or committee member", "I undertake, have undertaken or propose to undertake another role under the Act", "I exercise or perform, have exercised or performed, or propose to exercise or perform a power or function as a representative or committee member", "I exercise, have exercised or propose to exercise a power under the Act, or in a particular way", "I perform, have performed or propose to perform a function under the Act, or in a particular way", "I refrain, have refrained or propose to refrain from exercising a power or performing a function under the Act", "I assist, or give information to, someone exercising a power or performing a function under the Act", "I raise, have raised or propose to raise a work health and safety issue or concern with someone section 106(h) lists" (the business, an inspector, an entry permit holder, a representative, a committee member, another worker, anyone else with a duty or exercising a power or function under the Act), "I am, have been or propose to be involved in resolving a work health and safety issue under the Act", "I am taking, have taken or propose to take action to seek compliance with a duty or obligation under the Act", "none of these reasons"
      `I allege it was the reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you allege, in the proceeding, that the conduct was engaged in for that reason
      `the defendant proves it was not a substantial reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who acted proves, on the balance of probabilities, that the reason was not a substantial reason for the conduct
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is it taken to be for a prohibited reason` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the circumstance` EQUALS `none of these reasons` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (NOT `I allege it was the reason`) THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (silent: the presumption needs an allegation; the court decides otherwise)
           IF `the defendant proves it was not a substantial reason` THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 113(1) "A proceeding brought under section 112 must be commenced not more than 1 year after the date on which the
-- applicant knew or ought to have known that the cause of action accrued." The last day is the same day a year later
-- (29 February to 28 February; fork F1).
@export By what day must I start my claim in the Industrial Court for discriminatory or coercive conduct (s 113(1))?
GIVEN `the day I knew or ought to have known of the cause of action` IS A DATE @desc The day you knew, or ought to have known, that your cause of action accrued (usually the day of the conduct, or the day you learned of it)
GIVETH A DATE
`By what day must I apply to the Industrial Court` MEANS
    `the day I knew or ought to have known of the cause of action` `plus years` 1

-- s 112(1) "An eligible person may apply to the Industrial Court for an order"; s 112(6) the eligible persons are "(a) a
-- person affected by the contravention, (b) a person authorised as a representative by" one (a list not closed with
-- "only": NOTHING for anyone else, fork F7). s 113(1) the one-year period (JUST FALSE after it). s 115 "A person cannot
-- (a) commence a proceeding under Division 3 ... if the person has commenced a proceeding or made an application or
-- complaint in relation to the same matter under a law of the Commonwealth or a State and that proceeding, application
-- or complaint has not been withdrawn, ... (c) commence or continue an application under Division 3 ... if the person
-- has failed in a proceeding, application or complaint in relation to the same matter under a law of the Commonwealth
-- or a State, other than [one] relating to workers' compensation." Flat "cannot": JUST FALSE.
@export Can I apply to the Industrial Court for an order about discriminatory or coercive conduct against me, on the day I plan to (ss 112, 113(1), 115)?
GIVEN `my standing` IS A `My standing` @desc Who you are in the claim. One of: "I am a person affected by the contravention", "I am a representative authorised by a person affected", "neither"
      `the conduct I complain of` IS A `The conduct I complain of` @desc The conduct you complain of. One of: "discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason", "requesting, instructing, inducing, encouraging, authorising or assisting discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason", "coercion or inducement contrary to section 108" (action taken or threatened to make you use, or not use, a power, function or role under the Act), "none of these kinds of conduct"
      `the day I knew or ought to have known of the cause of action` IS A DATE @desc The day you knew, or ought to have known, that your cause of action accrued
      `the day I plan to apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you would file the application
      `I have another proceeding on the same matter not withdrawn` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have started a proceeding, application or complaint about the same matter under another Commonwealth or State law (for example an unfair dismissal or general protections claim) that has not been withdrawn
      `I failed in another proceeding on the same matter, not a workers compensation one` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed in a proceeding, application or complaint about the same matter under another Commonwealth or State law, other than one about workers compensation
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to the Industrial Court` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `I have another proceeding on the same matter not withdrawn` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `I failed in another proceeding on the same matter, not a workers compensation one` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (`By what day must I apply to the Industrial Court` `the day I knew or ought to have known of the cause of action`) `is before` `the day I plan to apply` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `my standing` EQUALS `neither` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 112(6) lists eligible persons without "only"; fork F7)
           IF `the conduct I complain of` EQUALS `none of these kinds of conduct` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (silent: s 112 gives no orders for other conduct, but does not bar the application)
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 113(3) "It is a defence to a proceeding under section 112 in relation to conduct referred to in section 112 (2) (a)
-- or (b) if the defendant proves that (a) the conduct was reasonable in the circumstances, and (b) a substantial reason
-- for the conduct was to comply with the requirements of this Act or a corresponding WHS law." Not proved: this section
-- gives no defence on these facts (JUST FALSE). For coercion (s 112(2)(c)) the subsection does not speak: NOTHING.
@export In my Industrial Court claim, does the person who acted have the defence that they acted reasonably to comply with the law (s 113(3))?
GIVEN `the conduct I complain of` IS A `The conduct I complain of` @desc The conduct you complain of. One of: "discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason", "requesting, instructing, inducing, encouraging, authorising or assisting discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason", "coercion or inducement contrary to section 108", "none of these kinds of conduct"
      `they prove the conduct was reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who acted proves that the conduct was reasonable in the circumstances
      `they prove a substantial reason was to comply with the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they prove that a substantial reason for the conduct was to comply with the requirements of the Act or a corresponding WHS law of another Australian jurisdiction
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the person who acted have the section 113(3) defence` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the conduct I complain of`
    WHEN `coercion or inducement contrary to section 108` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (scope limit: s 113(3) covers only s 112(2)(a), (b) conduct)
    WHEN `none of these kinds of conduct` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (scope limit, as above)
    OTHERWISE
        IF `they prove the conduct was reasonable` AND `they prove a substantial reason was to comply with the Act`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 112(3) the orders: "(a) an injunction, or (a1) an order declaring that the person has engaged in conduct of a type
-- referred to in subsection (2)(a), (b) or (c), or (b) in the case of conduct referred to in subsection (2) (a) or (b),
-- an order that the person pay (within a specified period) the compensation ... or (c) in the case of conduct referred
-- to in subsection (2) (a) in relation to a worker who was or is an employee or prospective employee, an order that
-- [reinstatement, re-employment or employment], or (d) any other order that the Industrial Court considers
-- appropriate." s 114(3): if a criminal court has made a s 111 order about the same (a) or (b) conduct, "the Industrial
-- Court cannot make an order under section 112" about it. s 115(b): a person cannot "recover any compensation under
-- Division 3 ... if the person has received compensation for the matter under a law of the Commonwealth or a State"
-- (read as taking the compensation order off the list, fork F8). The answer assumes the court finds the conduct proved.
@export If the Industrial Court finds the conduct proved, what orders can it make for me (ss 112(3), 114(3), 115(b))?
GIVEN `the conduct I complain of` IS A `The conduct I complain of` @desc The conduct the court finds. One of: "discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason", "requesting, instructing, inducing, encouraging, authorising or assisting discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason", "coercion or inducement contrary to section 108", "none of these kinds of conduct"
      `I was or am an employee or prospective employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were or are an employee (or prospective employee) of the person, rather than a contractor or other worker
      `I have received compensation for the matter under another law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already received compensation for the same matter under another Commonwealth or State law
      `a criminal court made a section 111 order for the same conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court, on convicting the person of the offence of discriminatory conduct, has already made a compensation or reinstatement order for the same conduct
GIVETH A LIST OF `Order`
`What orders can the Industrial Court make for me` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the conduct I complain of`
    WHEN `discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason` THEN
        IF `a criminal court made a section 111 order for the same conduct`
        THEN EMPTY
        ELSE `an injunction` FOLLOWED BY (`the declaration` FOLLOWED BY (`compensation` `followed by` (`reinstatement` `followed by` (LIST `any other order the Industrial Court considers appropriate`))))
    WHEN `requesting, instructing, inducing, encouraging, authorising or assisting discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason` THEN
        IF `a criminal court made a section 111 order for the same conduct`
        THEN EMPTY
        ELSE `an injunction` FOLLOWED BY (`the declaration` FOLLOWED BY (`compensation` `followed by` (LIST `any other order the Industrial Court considers appropriate`)))
    WHEN `coercion or inducement contrary to section 108` THEN
        LIST `an injunction`, `the declaration`, `any other order the Industrial Court considers appropriate`
    OTHERWISE EMPTY
    WHERE
        `the declaration` MEANS `an order declaring that the person engaged in the conduct`
        `compensation` MEANS
            IF `I have received compensation for the matter under another law` THEN NOTHING ELSE JUST `an order that the person pay me compensation within a specified period`
        `reinstatement` MEANS
            IF `I was or am an employee or prospective employee` THEN JUST `an order that I be reinstated or re-employed in my former or a similar position, or employed in the position I applied for or a similar one` ELSE NOTHING

-- Puts an optional order in front of a list.
GIVEN `the order` IS A MAYBE `Order`, `the rest` IS A LIST OF `Order`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Order`
`the order` `followed by` `the rest` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the order`
    WHEN JUST o THEN o FOLLOWED BY `the rest`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `the rest`

-- s 111 "If a person is convicted or found guilty of an offence under section 104 or 107, the court may (in addition to
-- imposing a penalty) make either or both of the following orders (a) [compensation to the person who was the subject
-- of the discriminatory conduct], (b) in relation to a person who was or is an employee or prospective employee,
-- [reinstatement, re-employment or employment]." s 114(2): if the Industrial Court has made a s 112 order about the same
-- conduct, "the court cannot make an order under section 111".
@export If the person who discriminated against me is convicted, what orders can the criminal court make for me (ss 111, 114(2))?
GIVEN `the person was convicted of the offence of discriminatory conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person has been convicted or found guilty of discriminatory conduct (s 104) or of requesting or assisting it (s 107)
      `I was or am an employee or prospective employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were or are an employee (or prospective employee) of the person
      `the Industrial Court made a section 112 order for the same conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Industrial Court has already made an order on your civil claim about the same conduct
GIVETH A LIST OF `Order`
`What orders can the criminal court make for me` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the person was convicted of the offence of discriminatory conduct`) OR `the Industrial Court made a section 112 order for the same conduct`
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE IF `I was or am an employee or prospective employee`
         THEN LIST `an order that the person pay me compensation within a specified period`, `an order that I be reinstated or re-employed in my former or a similar position, or employed in the position I applied for or a similar one`
         ELSE LIST `an order that the person pay me compensation within a specified period`

-- s 109(1) "A person must not knowingly or recklessly make a false or misleading representation to another person about
-- that other person's (a) rights or obligations under this Act, or (b) ability to initiate, or participate in, a
-- process or proceedings under this Act, or (c) ability to make a complaint or inquiry to a person or body empowered
-- under this Act to seek compliance with this Act." s 109(2) "Subsection (1) does not apply if the person to whom the
-- representation is made would not be expected to rely on it." A prohibition limited by its own words: outside the
-- limit, NOTHING; s 109(2) is a flat "does not apply": JUST FALSE.
@export Did the person who told me something false about my safety rights break the prohibition on misrepresentation (s 109)?
GIVEN `the statement was false or misleading` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what you were told was false or misleading
      `it was about my rights or my ability to complain or take part under the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was about your rights or obligations under the Act, your ability to start or take part in a process or proceeding under it, or your ability to complain or inquire to someone empowered to enforce it
      `they made it knowingly or recklessly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person knew it was false or misleading, or was reckless about that
      `I would be expected to rely on it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone in your position would be expected to rely on what you were told
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did they break the prohibition on misrepresentation` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `I would be expected to rely on it`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `the statement was false or misleading` AND `it was about my rights or my ability to complain or take part under the Act` AND `they made it knowingly or recklessly` THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a prohibition limited in its own words; the case is outside the limit)

§§ `Part 2 and Part 2A -- the offences that follow a breach (ss 30-34F)`

-- s 31 (Category 1): a health and safety duty; "without reasonable excuse, engages in conduct that exposes an
-- individual ... to a risk of death or serious injury or illness"; "with gross negligence" or "reckless as to the risk".
-- s 32 (Category 2): a duty; fails to comply; "the failure exposes an individual to a risk of death or serious injury or
-- illness". s 33 (Category 3): a duty; fails to comply. s 34C (industrial manslaughter): a duty; a business or an
-- officer of one; a failure to comply that "causes the death of a worker or another individual to whom the ... duty is
-- owed"; "with gross negligence". s 34(1) "A volunteer does not commit an offence under this Division for a failure to
-- comply with a health and safety duty, except a duty under section 28 or 29"; s 34D a volunteer does not commit
-- industrial manslaughter; s 34(2) an unincorporated association does not commit an offence for its own duty. Every
-- element is the court's finding, asked conditionally. s 34A (police responding to an armed offender) is out.
@export If the court finds these facts, what is the most serious offence the person whose conduct harmed me committed (ss 31-34, 34C, 34D)?
GIVEN `they had a health and safety duty to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person owed you a health and safety duty under Part 2 (as a business, an officer, a worker or another person at the workplace)
      `their duty was only as a worker or a person at the workplace` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the only duty in question is the duty of a worker (s 28) or of another person at the workplace (s 29)
      `they are a volunteer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is a volunteer
      `they are an unincorporated association` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is an unincorporated association (such as a club that is not incorporated)
      `they conduct the business or are an officer of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person conducts the business or undertaking, or is an officer of it
      `they failed to comply with the duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person failed to comply with the duty
      `the conduct exposed someone to a risk of death or serious injury or illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct or failure exposed you or another individual to a risk of death or serious injury or illness
      `the failure caused a death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the failure caused the death of a worker or another individual to whom the duty was owed
      `they were grossly negligent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds the person acted with gross negligence
      `they were reckless as to the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds the person was reckless as to the risk of death or serious injury or illness
      `they had no reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person acted without reasonable excuse (the prosecution must prove this for Category 1)
GIVETH A `The most serious offence on these facts`
`Which offence was it` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `industrial manslaughter` THEN `industrial manslaughter`
           IF `category 1` THEN `a Category 1 offence`
           IF `category 2` THEN `a Category 2 offence`
           IF `category 3` THEN `a Category 3 offence`
           OTHERWISE `no offence under Part 2 or Part 2A on these facts`
    WHERE
        `can offend` MEANS
                `they had a health and safety duty to me`
            AND (NOT `they are an unincorporated association`)
            AND (NOT (`they are a volunteer` AND (NOT `their duty was only as a worker or a person at the workplace`)))
        `industrial manslaughter` MEANS
                `can offend`
            AND (NOT `they are a volunteer`)
            AND `they conduct the business or are an officer of it`
            AND `they failed to comply with the duty`
            AND `the failure caused a death`
            AND `they were grossly negligent`
        `category 1` MEANS
                `can offend`
            AND `they had no reasonable excuse`
            AND `the conduct exposed someone to a risk of death or serious injury or illness`
            AND (`they were grossly negligent` OR `they were reckless as to the risk`)
        `category 2` MEANS
                `can offend`
            AND `they failed to comply with the duty`
            AND `the conduct exposed someone to a risk of death or serious injury or illness`
        `category 3` MEANS
                `can offend`
            AND `they failed to comply with the duty`

§§ `ss 229B, 242B, 242C -- penalties and the court`

-- s 242B(1) "the value of a penalty unit is (a) for the financial year 2019-20 -- $100, and (b) for each subsequent
-- financial year -- the amount calculated" by the CPI formula (lost in the deposited text). The editorial note under
-- s 242B gives the amounts to 2024-25: $102.00, $102.95, $107.47, $115.29, $120.42. A financial year is named by the
-- calendar year it starts in (1 July). Later years need a CPI figure not deposited, and the deposited version has no
-- penalty unit before 2019-20: NOTHING (fork F9).
GIVEN `the year it starts` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the value of a penalty unit in the financial year starting` `the year it starts` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the year it starts` EQUALS 2019 THEN JUST 100
           IF `the year it starts` EQUALS 2020 THEN JUST 102
           IF `the year it starts` EQUALS 2021 THEN JUST 102.95
           IF `the year it starts` EQUALS 2022 THEN JUST 107.47
           IF `the year it starts` EQUALS 2023 THEN JUST 115.29
           IF `the year it starts` EQUALS 2024 THEN JUST 120.42
           OTHERWISE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the sources: the CPI figures are not deposited)

-- The maximum penalty in penalty units, from each offence's "Maximum penalty". Industrial manslaughter has no penalty
-- in units: "(a) for an individual -- imprisonment for 25 years, or (b) for a body corporate -- $20,000,000".
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `The offence`, `the offender` IS A `The offender`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the maximum penalty units for` `the offence` `by` `the offender` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `industrial manslaughter (s 34C)` THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `a Category 1 offence (s 31)` THEN JUST (`by kind` 18805 9038 90424)
    WHEN `a Category 2 offence (s 32)` THEN JUST (`by kind` 3626 1813 18128)
    WHEN `a Category 3 offence (s 33)` THEN JUST (`by kind` 1214 607 6070)
    WHEN `failing to notify a notifiable incident (s 38(1))` THEN JUST (`by kind` 121 121 607)
    WHEN `failing to keep the record of a notifiable incident (s 38(7))` THEN JUST (`by kind` 61 61 304)
    WHEN `failing to preserve an incident site (s 39(1))` THEN JUST (`by kind` 121 121 607)
    WHEN `discriminatory conduct, or requesting or assisting it (s 104 or 107)` THEN JUST (`by kind` 1206 1206 6028)
    WHEN `coercion or inducement (s 108)` THEN JUST (`by kind` 1206 1206 6028)
    WHEN `misrepresentation about rights under the Act (s 109)` THEN JUST (`by kind` 1206 1206 6028)
    WHEN `levying a worker for health and safety (s 273)` THEN JUST (`by kind` 61 61 304)
    WHERE
        GIVEN `as the business or officer` IS A NUMBER, `as another individual` IS A NUMBER, `as a body corporate` IS A NUMBER
        GIVETH A NUMBER
        `by kind` `as the business or officer` `as another individual` `as a body corporate` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the offender`
            WHEN `an individual conducting the business or undertaking, or an officer of it` THEN `as the business or officer`
            WHEN `any other individual` THEN `as another individual`
            WHEN `a body corporate` THEN `as a body corporate`

-- s 242C(1) the dollars are the penalty units times the unit's value; (2) rounded down to a whole dollar.
GIVEN `the units` IS A NUMBER, `the unit value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the dollars for` `the units` `at` `the unit value` MEANS FLOOR (`the units` TIMES `the unit value`)

-- s 229B(4) "The maximum monetary penalty that may be imposed by the Local Court in proceedings for an offence against
-- this Act is an amount equal to the monetary value of 1,200 penalty units, despite any higher maximum monetary
-- penalty provided in respect of the offence." It applies to industrial manslaughter by a body corporate too, if dealt
-- with summarily in the Local Court (finding 4). The fine of an individual for industrial manslaughter: the Act sets
-- none (imprisonment only): NOTHING (fork F10).
@export What is the most the offender can be fined for the offence that harmed me, in dollars (ss 31-33, 34C, 38, 39, 104-109, 229B(4), 242B, 242C, 273)?
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `The offence` @desc The offence. One of: "industrial manslaughter (s 34C)", "a Category 1 offence (s 31)", "a Category 2 offence (s 32)", "a Category 3 offence (s 33)", "failing to notify a notifiable incident (s 38(1))", "failing to keep the record of a notifiable incident (s 38(7))", "failing to preserve an incident site (s 39(1))", "discriminatory conduct, or requesting or assisting it (s 104 or 107)", "coercion or inducement (s 108)", "misrepresentation about rights under the Act (s 109)", "levying a worker for health and safety (s 273)"
      `the offender` IS A `The offender` @desc Who is charged. One of: "an individual conducting the business or undertaking, or an officer of it", "any other individual" (for example a worker), "a body corporate"
      `the financial year it starts` IS A NUMBER @desc The calendar year in which the financial year of the penalty starts (1 July), for example 2024 for 2024-25
      `the case is heard in the Local Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the prosecution is dealt with in the Local Court
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the most the offender can be fined` MEANS
    IF `the offence` EQUALS `industrial manslaughter (s 34C)`
    THEN IF `the offender` EQUALS `a body corporate`
         THEN IF `the case is heard in the Local Court`
              THEN `the Local Court cap of` 20000000
              ELSE JUST 20000000
         ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (silent: s 34C sets imprisonment only for an individual; fork F10)
    ELSE CONSIDER `the maximum penalty units for` `the offence` `by` `the offender`
         WHEN JUST units THEN
             CONSIDER `the value of a penalty unit in the financial year starting` `the financial year it starts`
             WHEN JUST v THEN
                 IF `the case is heard in the Local Court`
                 THEN JUST (`the smaller of` (`the dollars for` units `at` v) `and` (`the dollars for` 1200 `at` v))
                 ELSE JUST (`the dollars for` units `at` v)
             WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the sources; fork F9)
         WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHERE
        GIVEN `the dollars` IS A NUMBER
        GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
        `the Local Court cap of` `the dollars` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the value of a penalty unit in the financial year starting` `the financial year it starts`
            WHEN JUST v THEN JUST (`the smaller of` `the dollars` `and` (`the dollars for` 1200 `at` v))
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the sources; fork F9)

-- The longest imprisonment: s 31 "10 years imprisonment" for an individual; s 34C "imprisonment for 25 years" for an
-- individual; no other offence here carries imprisonment (0).
@export What is the longest prison term, in years, the offender faces for the offence that harmed me (ss 31, 34C)?
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `The offence` @desc The offence (as in the fine question)
      `the offender` IS A `The offender` @desc Who is charged. One of: "an individual conducting the business or undertaking, or an officer of it", "any other individual", "a body corporate"
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the longest prison term` MEANS
    IF `the offender` EQUALS `a body corporate`
    THEN 0
    ELSE CONSIDER `the offence`
         WHEN `industrial manslaughter (s 34C)` THEN 25
         WHEN `a Category 1 offence (s 31)` THEN 10
         OTHERWISE 0

-- s 229B(1) "Except as provided by this section, proceedings for an offence ... must be dealt with summarily before
-- (a) the Local Court, or (b) the Industrial Court." (2) industrial manslaughter by an individual and (3) a Category 1
-- offence by an individual "must be dealt with on indictment". (3A) those two offences by a body corporate "must be
-- dealt with summarily unless the prosecutor elects" indictment.
@export Which court deals with the prosecution for the offence that harmed me (s 229B)?
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `The offence` @desc The offence (as in the fine question)
      `the offender` IS A `The offender` @desc Who is charged. One of: "an individual conducting the business or undertaking, or an officer of it", "any other individual", "a body corporate"
GIVETH A `How the prosecution is dealt with`
`Which court deals with the prosecution` MEANS
    IF `the offence` EQUALS `industrial manslaughter (s 34C)` OR `the offence` EQUALS `a Category 1 offence (s 31)`
    THEN IF `the offender` EQUALS `a body corporate`
         THEN `summarily, before the Local Court or the Industrial Court, unless the prosecutor elects to proceed on indictment`
         ELSE `on indictment`
    ELSE `summarily, before the Local Court or the Industrial Court`

§§ `Part 13 -- prosecutions (ss 230-232, 34E)`

-- s 230(1) proceedings "may only be brought by (a) the regulator, or (b) an inspector [authorised], or (c) the secretary
-- of an industrial organisation of employees any member or members of which are concerned in the matter to which the
-- proceedings relate, but only as permitted by subsection (3) if the offence concerned is a Category 1 offence or a
-- Category 2 offence." (3) for those, only if the regulator "has (after referral of the matter to the regulator and the
-- Director of Public Prosecutions under section 231) declined to follow the advice of the Director of Public
-- Prosecutions to bring the proceedings". A list closed with "only": JUST FALSE outside it. Industrial manslaughter is
-- not a Category 1 or 2 offence, so (3) does not limit it (finding 5).
@export Can the secretary of my union bring the prosecution for the offence that harmed me (s 230)?
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `The offence` @desc The offence (as in the fine question)
      `members of the union are concerned in the matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether one or more members of the union (an industrial organisation of employees), such as you, are concerned in the matter
      `the regulator declined to follow the DPP's advice to prosecute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, after a referral under s 231, the regulator declined to follow the Director of Public Prosecutions' advice to bring the proceedings
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my union bring the prosecution` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `members of the union are concerned in the matter`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `the offence` EQUALS `a Category 1 offence (s 31)` OR `the offence` EQUALS `a Category 2 offence (s 32)`
           THEN IF `the regulator declined to follow the DPP's advice to prosecute` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 231(1) "If (a) a person reasonably considers that the occurrence of an act, matter or thing constitutes a Category
-- 1 offence or a Category 2 offence, and (b) no prosecution has been brought in relation to the occurrence ... after 6
-- months but not later than 18 months after that occurrence, the person may make a written request to the regulator
-- that a prosecution be brought." The window opens the day after six months end and closes on the last day of 18
-- months (fork F11).
@export From what day can I ask the regulator in writing to prosecute the Category 1 or 2 offence that harmed me (s 231(1))?
GIVEN `the day it happened` IS A DATE @desc The day the act, matter or thing (or failure) happened
GIVETH A DATE
`From what day can I ask the regulator to prosecute` MEANS
    (`the day it happened` `plus months` 6) `plus days` 1

@export What is the last day I can ask the regulator in writing to prosecute the Category 1 or 2 offence that harmed me (s 231(1))?
GIVEN `the day it happened` IS A DATE @desc The day the act, matter or thing (or failure) happened
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the last day I can ask the regulator to prosecute` MEANS
    `the day it happened` `plus months` 18

@export Can I ask the regulator in writing to prosecute, on the day I plan to (s 231(1))?
GIVEN `I reasonably consider it a Category 1 or 2 offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you reasonably consider that what happened was a Category 1 offence (reckless or grossly negligent exposure to risk of death or serious injury) or a Category 2 offence (a failure of a safety duty exposing someone to that risk)
      `a prosecution has been brought` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a prosecution has already been brought about it
      `the day it happened` IS A DATE @desc The day the act, matter or thing (or failure) happened
      `the day I plan to ask` IS A DATE @desc The day you would make the written request
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I ask the regulator to prosecute` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `I reasonably consider it a Category 1 or 2 offence`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `a prosecution has been brought` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `the day I plan to ask` `is before` (`From what day can I ask the regulator to prosecute` `the day it happened`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (`What is the last day I can ask the regulator to prosecute` `the day it happened`) `is before` `the day I plan to ask` THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 231(2) "Within 3 months after the regulator receives a request the regulator must (a) advise the person (in
-- writing) (i) whether the investigation is complete, and (ii) if the investigation is complete, whether a prosecution
-- has been or will be brought or give reasons why a prosecution will not be brought". Time 0 is the day the regulator
-- received the request; the unit is days; the last day is the same day three months on (fork F1).
GIVEN `the days in the three months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `WHS party` `WHS act`
`s 231(2) -- the regulator must advise the person within 3 months of receiving the request` `the days in the three months` MEANS
    PARTY `the regulator`
    MUST `advise the person who asked in writing`
    WITHIN `the days in the three months`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the regulator` BECAUSE "the person who asked for a prosecution was not advised in writing within 3 months after the regulator received the request (s 231(2))"

@export Has the regulator answered my written request for a prosecution in time (s 231(2))?
GIVEN `the day the regulator received my request` IS A DATE @desc The day the regulator received your written request
      `the regulator has advised me in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the regulator has advised you in writing whether the investigation is complete (and, if it is, whether a prosecution will be brought, or why not)
      `the day the regulator advised me` IS A DATE @desc If it has, the day of that advice (any day otherwise)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (usually today)
GIVETH A `Whether it was done in time`
`Has the regulator answered my request in time` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 231(2) -- the regulator must advise the person within 3 months of receiving the request` `the three months`) 0 `the events`
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN `done in time`
    OTHERWISE
        BRANCH IF `the regulator has advised me in writing` THEN `done late`
               IF `the days to today` GREATER THAN `the three months` THEN `not done, and the time has passed`
               OTHERWISE `not done yet, and the time has not passed`
    WHERE
        `the three months` MEANS `the days from` `the day the regulator received my request` `to` (`the day the regulator received my request` `plus months` 3)
        `the days to today` MEANS `the days from` `the day the regulator received my request` `to` `today`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `the regulator has advised me in writing`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the regulator` `advise the person who asked in writing` (`the days from` `the day the regulator received my request` `to` `the day the regulator advised me`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL `the days to today`)

-- s 231(3) if the regulator advises that a prosecution for a Category 1 or 2 offence will not be brought, it must
-- "(b) if the person makes a written request to the regulator to do so, refer the matter to the Director of Public
-- Prosecutions within 1 month of the request." s 231(4) "The Director of Public Prosecutions must consider the matter
-- and advise (in writing) the regulator within 1 month" (from the referral, fork F12); s 231(5) a copy of the advice
-- goes to the person who asked.
@export If the regulator will not prosecute and I ask in writing for the matter to go to the Director of Public Prosecutions, by what day must the regulator refer it (s 231(3)(b))?
GIVEN `the day I asked for the referral` IS A DATE @desc The day you made the written request to refer the matter to the Director of Public Prosecutions
GIVETH A DATE
`By what day must the regulator refer my matter to the DPP` MEANS
    `the day I asked for the referral` `plus months` 1

@export Once my matter is referred, by what day must the Director of Public Prosecutions advise the regulator (a copy coming to me) (s 231(4), (5))?
GIVEN `the day the matter was referred` IS A DATE @desc The day the regulator referred the matter to the Director of Public Prosecutions
GIVETH A DATE
`By what day must the DPP advise the regulator` MEANS
    `the day the matter was referred` `plus months` 1

-- s 232(1) "Proceedings for an offence against this Act may be brought within the latest of the following periods to
-- occur (a) within 2 years after the offence first comes to the notice of the regulator, (b) within 1 year after a
-- coronial report was made or a coronial inquiry or inquest ended, if it appeared from the report or the proceedings
-- ... that an offence had been committed against this Act, (c) if a WHS undertaking has been given in relation to the
-- offence, within 6 months after (i) the WHS undertaking is contravened, or (ii) it comes to the notice of the
-- regulator that the WHS undertaking has been contravened, or (iii) the regulator has agreed under section 221 to the
-- withdrawal of the WHS undertaking." (2) a Category 1 prosecution may be brought after the period "if fresh evidence
-- relevant to the offence is discovered and the court is satisfied that the evidence could not reasonably have been
-- discovered within the relevant limitation period". s 34E: industrial manslaughter proceedings "may be commenced at
-- any time".
@export By what day must a prosecution for the offence that harmed me be brought (ss 232, 34E)?
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `The offence` @desc The offence (as in the fine question)
      `the day the regulator first knew of the offence` IS A DATE @desc The day the offence first came to the notice of the regulator (SafeWork NSW)
      `a coronial report or inquest showed an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner's report was made, or a coronial inquiry or inquest ended, from which it appeared an offence against the Act had been committed
      `the day of the coronial report or the end of the inquest` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day the report was made or the inquiry or inquest ended (any day otherwise)
      `a WHS undertaking was contravened or withdrawn` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an enforceable undertaking was given about the offence and has since been contravened, or withdrawn with the regulator's agreement
      `the latest day of the undertaking's contravention, its notice or its withdrawal` IS A DATE @desc If so, the latest of: the day it was contravened, the day the regulator learned of that, and the day the regulator agreed to its withdrawal (any day otherwise)
      `the court accepts fresh evidence for a Category 1 offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether fresh evidence has been discovered that the court is satisfied could not reasonably have been discovered within the period (this matters only for a Category 1 offence)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (usually today)
GIVETH A `The time limit for the prosecution`
`By what day must a prosecution be brought` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the offence` EQUALS `industrial manslaughter (s 34C)` THEN `there is no time limit, because it is industrial manslaughter`
           IF     `the offence` EQUALS `a Category 1 offence (s 31)`
              AND `the court accepts fresh evidence for a Category 1 offence`
              AND `the last day` `is before` `today`
           THEN `the period has ended, but a Category 1 prosecution may still be brought on fresh evidence the court accepts`
           OTHERWISE `the prosecution must be brought by` `the last day`
    WHERE
        `the regulator's two years` MEANS `the day the regulator first knew of the offence` `plus years` 2
        `with the coroner` MEANS
            IF `a coronial report or inquest showed an offence`
            THEN `the later of` `the regulator's two years` `and` (`the day of the coronial report or the end of the inquest` `plus years` 1)
            ELSE `the regulator's two years`
        `the last day` MEANS
            IF `a WHS undertaking was contravened or withdrawn`
            THEN `the later of` `with the coroner` `and` (`the latest day of the undertaking's contravention, its notice or its withdrawal` `plus months` 6)
            ELSE `with the coroner`

§§ `s 267 and Part 14 -- civil claims, contracts and charges`

-- s 267 "Except as provided in Part 6 and Part 7 and Division 7 of this Part, nothing in this Act is to be construed as
-- (a) conferring a right of action in civil proceedings in relation to a contravention of a provision of this Act, or
-- (b) conferring a defence to an action in civil proceedings or otherwise affecting a right of action in civil
-- proceedings". Part 6 gives the worker the s 112 application; Part 7 and Division 7 are the regulator's and
-- inspectors' civil penalty proceedings (s 260), not the worker's. Elsewhere a flat negative: JUST FALSE. The worker's
-- claim for damages in negligence or under the workers compensation Acts is untouched (s 267(b)).
@export Does the Work Health and Safety Act itself give me a right to sue over this breach of it (s 267)?
GIVEN `the breach is discriminatory or coercive conduct under Part 6` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the breach you complain of is discriminatory conduct, inducing it, or coercion (Part 6), rather than a breach of a safety duty or another provision
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does this Act give me a right to sue for the breach` MEANS
    IF `the breach is discriminatory or coercive conduct under Part 6`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 272 "A term of any agreement or contract that purports to exclude, limit or modify the operation of this Act or any
-- duty owed under this Act or to transfer to another person any duty owed under this Act is void." Another term: the
-- section is silent (NOTHING).
@export Is this term of my contract or agreement void because it tries to contract out of the Act (s 272)?
GIVEN `the term excludes, limits or modifies the Act or a duty, or transfers a duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the term purports to exclude, limit or modify the operation of the Act or a duty under it, or to transfer a duty under it to someone else (for example making you responsible for your own safety equipment instead of the business)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is this term of my contract void` MEANS
    IF `the term excludes, limits or modifies the Act or a duty, or transfers a duty`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (silent: s 272 says nothing of other terms)

-- s 273 "A person conducting a business or undertaking must not impose a levy or charge on a worker, or permit a levy
-- or charge to be imposed on a worker, for anything done, or provided, in relation to work health and safety." A flat
-- "must not": JUST FALSE. A charge for something else, or on someone not its worker: NOTHING.
@export Can the business I work for charge me for something done or provided for health and safety, such as protective equipment or training (s 273)?
GIVEN `I am a worker of the business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you carry out work for the business or undertaking that would charge you
      `the charge is for something done or provided for work health and safety` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the levy or charge is for anything done or provided in relation to work health and safety (such as protective equipment, safety training or health monitoring)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the business charge me for health and safety` MEANS
    IF `I am a worker of the business` AND `the charge is for something done or provided for work health and safety`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a prohibition limited in its own words; the case is outside the limit)
